Cultured Code today released Things 3.25 for Mac, bringing full optimization for the PROLED <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> to its task management app.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/things-touch-hero-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1440" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1078504" /><br/>
Since the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/how-to/disable-touch-macbook-pro-proled-xdr-sidecar/">touchscreen MacBook Pro</a> launched, all Mac apps feature basic touch input, with taps performing the equivalent of mouse clicks. Things already worked with that default behavior, but Cultured Code says version 3.25 fully optimizes the app for touch with additional gestures, improved dragging, and larger controls.<br/>
<br/>
A rightward swipe on a to-do opens the scheduling dialog, while a leftward swipe selects or deselects it. Users can also select several items by moving two fingers up or down through a list. To open a to-do, users can tap it to select it and tap again to expand it, or double-tap.<br/>
<br/>
Cultured Code says dragging to-dos and headings is smoother and more responsive, whether moving one item or a group. Holding a finger on an item in a selection picks up the whole group, rather than only that item. The update also improves how dragging works when some selected items are outside the visible area of the list.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/things-touch-swipe.jpg" alt="" width="2048" height="1152" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1078505" /><br/>
<br/>
Custom menus and date pickers adapt to the input method, showing more spacious rows and larger touch targets when opened with a finger. Buttons elsewhere in the app also have larger tap targets.<br/>
<br/>
Other changes make it easier to resize the sidebar by touch and rearrange checklist entries within a to-do. Users can cancel either a to-do or an individual checklist entry by pressing and holding its checkbox. The release also includes smaller adjustments to touch interactions and interface polish throughout the app.<br/>
<br/>
